<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Maihar State</h1><p class="st-meta">1778 CE – 1950 CE (approx.) · capital Maihar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kachhwaha Rajput state of Baghelkhand, home of the Sharda Devi hill temple and of the Maihar school of Hindustani music.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The ruling family of Maihar were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwaha Rajputs</a> who had served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundela">Bundela</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/panna-state">Panna</a> and received lands in the area. Around 1778 they established Maihar as their own principality, and the British confirmed it by sanad in the early 19th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Maihar was a 9-gun salute state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghelkhand-agency">Baghelkhand Agency</a>, ruled by a Raja. The goddess Sharda's temple on Trikuta hill drew pilgrims from across north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Raja Brijnath Singh made the sarod master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allauddin-khan">Allauddin Khan</a> his court musician, and pupils such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi-shankar">Ravi Shankar</a> and Ali <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> Khan trained there, creating the Maihar gharana.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After independence Maihar was merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vindhya-pradesh">Vindhya Pradesh</a> in 1948 and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/maihar-state.jpg" alt="Maihar State" width="320" height="263"><figcaption>Image: Imperial Gazetteer of India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_India_Agency_Map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1778 CE – 1950 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maihar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Brijnath Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bagheli</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maihar</dd></dl></aside></div>
